HI Huizi,

Thanks for your interest in Canopy! For CNA in cancer, especially overlapping CNAs, it is non-trivial to call accurately and this is important for Canopy in that otherwise it would be garbage in garbage out. Have you looked at the pages below?

https://github.com/yuchaojiang/Canopy/blob/master/instruction/SNA_CNA_input.md <https://urldefense.proofpoint.com/v2/url?u=https-3A__github.com_yuchaojiang_Canopy_blob_master_instruction_SNA-5FCNA-5Finput.md&d=DwMFAg&c=k9MF1d71ITtkuJx-PdWme51dKbmfPEvxwt8SFEkBfs4&r=cm0gCJLll2G0kLgFLODh9isuXPuIKp94acYMSJiBABI&m=CBoZsU4yU1w48ORa8amClHaubaW0pzkGApCc3H_Zfmo&s=b7ObJXKFaL-kw_hXAMiQm1n8rYc0Vfg8Iq-O037DmH0&e=> This is how to generate CNAs by FALCON and Sequenza. I believe there is QC included in the demo code by FALCON to curate the segmentation results although it is recommended that you also examine the results to make sure it makes sense.

https://github.com/yuchaojiang/Canopy/blob/master/instruction/SNA_CNA_choice.md <https://urldefense.proofpoint.com/v2/url?u=https-3A__github.com_yuchaojiang_Canopy_blob_master_instruction_SNA-5FCNA-5Fchoice.md&d=DwMFAg&c=k9MF1d71ITtkuJx-PdWme51dKbmfPEvxwt8SFEkBfs4&r=cm0gCJLll2G0kLgFLODh9isuXPuIKp94acYMSJiBABI&m=CBoZsU4yU1w48ORa8amClHaubaW0pzkGApCc3H_Zfmo&s=ytOvx_vVDvcN1PabLOXWonMFo8pYZGt06162oIqCG-g&e=>  on how to choose CNAs (basically, it is recommended to load FALCON or FALCON-X’s segmentation results into IGV for visualization and focus on the large (sometimes even chromosomal arm level) CNAs and the CNAs that show distinct patterns between the samples from the same patient).

https://github.com/yuchaojiang/Canopy/blob/master/instruction/overlapping_CNA.md <https://urldefense.proofpoint.com/v2/url?u=https-3A__github.com_yuchaojiang_Canopy_blob_master_instruction_overlapping-5FCNA.md&d=DwMFAg&c=k9MF1d71ITtkuJx-PdWme51dKbmfPEvxwt8SFEkBfs4&r=cm0gCJLll2G0kLgFLODh9isuXPuIKp94acYMSJiBABI&m=CBoZsU4yU1w48ORa8amClHaubaW0pzkGApCc3H_Zfmo&s=6M4dOE4qrKFHT_06-9g6s2KJyC-f-0W3iK20iefANvo&e=>  This is how to assign values in the C matrix for overlapping CNAs. In this case, you need to visualize the segmentation results otherwise it wouldn’t be identifiable (i.e., a one-copy loss can be a one-copy gain followed by a two-copy loss –the latter (two overlapping events) is mathematically not separable from the former (one event) plus biologically it is rare too). Therefore, at this stage, this still requires a lot of manual picking and usually this works better than automation. For example, a homozygous deletion within a heterozygous deletion will give you four breakpoints and three levels of normalized results (normal, 1-copy loss, and 2-copy loss). In this case, you know there are two CNV events.

I hope this address some of your questions. Feel free to let us know if you have more!

Yuchao

